date: 2026-09-10; host Darwin 25.5.0 aarch64 (mac) for the encode-level numbers,
  four hosts for the kernel numbers (`cbrt_candidates_2026-09-10.*`).

WHAT SHIPPED: the forward XYB cube root is now SELECTED, not fixed.
  * `EncoderStrategy::Libjxl` -> `XybCubeRoot::Libjxl`, a transcription of
    libjxl `base/fast_math-inl.h::CubeRootAndAdd`. Bit-exactness with the
    reference is the point of that strategy.
  * zen strategies -> `XybCubeRoot::MidP` (`magetypes::cbrt_midp`, 3 ULP),
    named once as `vardct::xyb::ZEN_XYB_CBRT`.
  Gate `xyb_cbrt_libjxl_parity`, Section D. Verified LIVE, not assumed: the two
  modes produce different bytes on all four probed cells, so the selector really
  reaches the kernel (the LZ77-hash null of 2026-09-08 is the reason this check
  is mandatory).

The replaced implementation ran two Newton iterations in **f64**, each with a
DIVISION, and extracted every lane to scalar for its bit-hack guess. It was
neither libjxl's algorithm nor fast.

WHY MidP AND NOT THE FASTEST. `cbrt_lowp` is the fastest on all four hosts, and
it is NOT taken. Measured at the ENCODE level, e3 threads=1, 15 reps, 6 images:
LowP is **0.984x** MidP (1.6 % faster), consistent across all six (0.979-0.992).
That is the only band where it shows — e5/e7/e9 are inside noise — and it costs
259 ULP against MidP's 3 (86x the error) on values that feed quantisation and
the perceptual loop. `ZEN_XYB_CBRT` is one constant, so the trade is one line
away if the owner wants it.

RD: `just rd-regression` green on ALL THREE variants (MidP, LowP, Libjxl), with
per-image SSIM2 differing by <= 0.09 between them — no measurable quality
separation on that gate.

ENCODE WALL vs cjxl v0.12, 1024^2 d=1, before -> after this change:
  e3 t1  1.14 -> 1.05      e3 t8  0.93 -> 0.89
  e5 t1  1.02 -> 1.00      e5 t8  1.20 -> 1.18
  e7 t1  0.97 -> 0.97      e7 t8  1.04 -> 1.02
  e9 t1  1.03 -> 1.03      e9 t8  0.46 -> 0.47
(These "before" figures are the same-run subset at d=1.0 only, so they do not
equal the both-distance medians in `ladder_vs_cjxl_after_2026-09-10.meta`.)

SIDE EFFECT WORTH MORE THAN THE SPEED: every `XybCubeRoot` variant is pure f32,
so the kernel no longer needs `f64x4` — and **the `v4`/AVX-512 tier is back in
its `incant!` list**, having been excluded precisely because magetypes has no
`F64x4Backend` for `X64V4Token`. Not measured on an AVX-512 host in this change.

BYTES: 7 of 63 hash-lock cells moved, all lossy (lossless never touches XYB —
a correctness signal, not a coincidence). Deltas 0 to -236 bytes on 58 KB-330 KB
files, i.e. <= 0.07 %. 0 cells added or removed. The Libjxl byte lock (5 cells)
did NOT move.

VERIFIED: full release suite (1586 + 507 + ...), `--features "__expert
__internals"` it-suite 527, workspace all-targets clippy, divergence drift 7/7
(new Section D row + `extract_anchors` fallback), djxl decode 20/20 incl. 1x1 and
259x133, thread determinism 0 mismatches over t {1,3,8}.

TRAPS HIT: (1) `UPDATE_HASHES=1` without `rm -f` APPENDS a second full set, and
without `--test-threads=1` it produces a TORN file with interleaved half-lines.
`just update-hashes` does both correctly — use it. (2) The dispatch-parity test
now loops over ALL THREE cube roots; a tier that agreed on `MidP` but not
`Libjxl` would otherwise have shipped silently to Libjxl-strategy users only.
